# AI Code Generation Guardrails

> Rules to prevent AI from generating unmaintainable Java code.

## Code Organization

### File Structure
- One top-level class/record/enum per file. Nested inner classes are fine when they are private implementation details and not used as field types in other classes
- Group by feature/domain, not by type
- Max 300 lines per file (split if larger)

### Naming
- Descriptive names over abbreviations (`getUserById` not `getUsr`)
- Never name variables, fields, or parameters with identifiers that shadow or resemble Java keywords or common type names: `record`, `return`, `class`, `value`, `object`, `string`, `list`. Use domain-qualified names instead (e.g., `bonusRecord`, `returnAmount`, `resultList`)
- Boolean variables start with `is`, `has`, `can`, `should`
- Methods describe actions: `calculateTotal`, `validateEmail`
- Constants in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Prefix unused parameters and variables with `_` (ErrorProne-compatible, e.g., `_testName`)
- Java 22+: use the unnamed variable `_` (JEP 456) when the project upgrades past Java 21

### Methods
- Single responsibility: one method does one thing
- Prefer method references over lambdas when possible (e.g., `Bonus::getId` not `b -> b.getId()`)

## Correctness

### Types and Precision
- `BigDecimal` for **all** monetary amounts and financial calculations -- no exceptions
- Never call `.doubleValue()`, `.floatValue()`, or `.toDouble()` on monetary BigDecimals
- When external APIs provide `double` (e.g., Avro), convert to `BigDecimal` at the boundary immediately via `BigDecimal.valueOf()`; do not propagate `double` through business logic
- Use `BigDecimal` comparison via `.compareTo()`, never `.equals()` (scale-sensitive)
- `Instant` or `LocalDateTime` for dates
- Use enums for fixed sets of values
- Prefer enums over string literals or boolean flags

## Complexity Control

### Keep It Flat
- Max 3 levels of nesting
- Max 15 cyclomatic complexity per method
- No nested ternaries
- Long boolean expressions → extract to named variable

### No Duplication (DRY)
- Copy-paste = extract to method
- Similar code in 2+ classes/files → shared utility
- Config values in one place
- When the same sequence of operations appears 2+ times, extract it into a named helper method
- This applies to any repeated pattern: stream/filter/map pipelines, builder chains, validation sequences, mapping logic, repository lookups with error handling
- The extracted method name should describe the **intent**, not the mechanics (e.g., `findActiveBonusByIdOrThrow()` not `findAndCheck()`)
- Prefer small, focused methods over inline lambdas when the logic is reused

## Testability

### Write Testable Code
- Pure functions where possible
- Dependency injection over hardcoding
- Small, focused units
- Avoid side effects in business logic

## Architecture

### Separation of Concerns
- Controllers/listeners handle I/O only (routing, request/response, deserialization)
- Business logic in dedicated services
- Data access separate from business logic
- Dependencies flow one direction (controller → service → repository)

## Documentation

### Required Comments
- WHY, not WHAT (code shows what, comments explain why)
- Complex algorithms get explanations
- Non-obvious business rules documented
- TODO format: `// TODO(username): description with ticket number/link`

### Self-Documenting Code
- Prefer clear names over comments
- Extract magic numbers to named constants
- Use records and sealed types to encode invariants

## Error Handling

### Always Handle Errors
- Never swallow errors silently
- Log with context (what was attempted, with what data)
- User-facing errors must be friendly
- System errors need debugging info

### Fail Fast
- Validate inputs at boundaries
- Return early on invalid state
- Use assertions for "should never happen"

## Consistency

### Follow Existing Patterns
- Match the style of surrounding code
- Use existing utilities before creating new
- Respect project conventions
- When in doubt, check similar files

### No Premature Optimization
- Make it work, make it right, make it fast (in that order)
- Profile before optimizing
- Optimize bottlenecks, not everything

## Logging

- Use structured arguments like `StructuredArguments.kv(BET_REFERENCE, betReference)` or use `Kv.betReference(betReference)` / `LoggingUtils.Structured.betReference(betReference)` if such are used in project
